From 61f4826ade2be049efca32a06cf52babe3233bdb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 00:37:01 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] mt-discovery-via-tenant-runtime: 4 follow-up fixes for
 end-to-end correctness
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch19's original commit (453d1c0fc) routed ListModelsRequest through
the tenant runtime — necessary but not sufficient. Live smoke testing
on the bm cluster revealed four more cross-tenant data-corruption
hazards along the same theme (process-shared state mutated by per-
tenant admin writes). All four are fixed here as a single follow-up
commit so the patch19 series ships end-to-end working.

These are also what the live `bash booststrap.sh && curl -X POST
/v1/chat/completions` test verifies — bare `qwen3.6-coder` now
auto-resolves and dispatches successfully through the chain.

Fix 1 — eager evict in attemptModelDiscovery
---------------------------------------------
transports/bifrost-http/handlers/providers.go::attemptModelDiscovery

The multitenant.Manager caches per-tenant runtimes. When a key
handler fires attemptModelDiscovery → ReloadProvider → BifrostFor,
BifrostFor returns the CACHED runtime — built from a configstore
snapshot taken BEFORE the just-POSTed key existed. So discovery
ran against a key-less account → "no keys found" → catalog stays
empty even though the DB has the key.

The handler's `defer EvictTenant` fires at function exit, too late.
Eager-evict inside attemptModelDiscovery (right after stamping the
tenant on baseCtx) so the next BifrostFor rebuilds the runtime from
a fresh DB snapshot that includes the just-POSTed key. The defer
still runs at function exit and is harmless when called twice
(Manager.Evict is a no-op when no runtime is cached).

Fix 2 — same fix on reloadProviderAfterCreate
---------------------------------------------
transports/bifrost-http/handlers/providers.go::reloadProviderAfterCreate

The provider-create path also called context.WithTimeout(
context.Background(), ...) for its discovery call — dropping the
tenant id. With no tenant in ctx, patch19's tid extraction in
ReloadProvider falls back to "" → BifrostFor returns the ROOT
client (defeating patch19). Mirror attemptModelDiscovery's pattern:
preserve tenant from request ctx, eager-evict, then bound the
timeout off the tenant-stamped base ctx.

Fix 3 — cross-tenant-safe DeleteModelDataForProvider in RemoveProvider
----------------------------------------------------------------------
transports/bifrost-http/server/server.go::RemoveProvider

When one tenant DELETEs a provider, server.go's RemoveProvider
calls modelCatalog.DeleteModelDataForProvider(name) — a tenant-
blind `delete(mc.modelPool, name)`. This wipes the catalog entry
for every other tenant that shares the provider name. Their
auto-resolve subsequently breaks because GetProvidersForModel
intersects the catalog with c.Providers and finds no qwen entry.

Check the configstore directly (with a context.Background() to
bypass the GORM tenant scope) and skip DeleteModelDataForProvider
when any other tenant still owns the provider name.

Fix 4 — cross-tenant-safe in-memory map delete in lib.Config.RemoveProvider
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
transports/bifrost-http/lib/config.go::RemoveProvider

Same shape as fix 3 but on `delete(c.Providers, provider)` — the
shared in-memory provider map. GetProvidersForModel intersects the
catalog against c.Providers; if c.Providers loses the entry, the
intersection is empty and auto-resolve fails even with a populated
catalog. Add the same "other tenants still own this name → preserve"
guard before the in-memory delete.

Fix 5 — skip UpsertModelData on discovery error for custom providers
--------------------------------------------------------------------
transports/bifrost-http/server/server.go::ReloadProvider

The seeder's DELETE pass on a tenant deletes the provider's KEY
first, then the provider. The key DELETE handler also fires
attemptModelDiscovery → ReloadProvider → no keys → ListModels
returns "no keys found" → bifrostErr set → upstream fallback zeros
allModels → UpsertModelDataForProvider called with empty data.

For built-in providers, the fallback "modelData empty → use
providerModels from pricingData" leaves modelPool[p] populated
(pricing has built-in providers). For custom providers, pricing
has no entries → modelPool[p] = []. The wipe propagates to every
other tenant via the shared modelPool. Skip the Upsert when
bifrostErr is set AND isCustom — leave the catalog as-is so a
previously-successful upsert from another tenant survives the
key-DELETE side effect.

Why these fixes belong in patch19
---------------------------------
All four share the same root cause patch19 addresses: process-shared
state (manager runtime cache, in-memory provider map, catalog
modelPool) mutated by per-tenant admin writes without per-tenant
guards. Patch19's routing change alone makes discovery USE the
tenant runtime, but doesn't stop other paths from clobbering that
runtime's outputs in the shared catalog.

Live smoke test result on the bm cluster
----------------------------------------
After this commit, with a clean seed:
- shaun's qwen key status=success ✓
- modelPool["qwen"] has qwen3.6-coder ✓ (filtered + unfiltered)
- POST /v1/chat/completions with bare "qwen3.6-coder" + tenant
  header succeeds end-to-end through vLLM ✓
- All isolation assertions in the seeder still pass ✓

NOT in scope
------------
The two boot-time loops (populateModelPoolWithListModels + the loop
in RegisterAPIRoutes around line 1530) still iterate s.Config.Providers
and use s.Client. They run BEFORE the seeder, so with patch18 A's
default-exclusion they iterate an empty map → no-op. If a cluster
boots with non-default tenants whose providers were loaded into
the shared map, those loops would still have the cross-tenant
issues. Best handled as a follow-up that either skips them in
strict-MT mode or iterates tenants explicitly.

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
